WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.429
A useful playbook has a predictable anatomy. The exact template can vary, but the core sections should be consistent enough that responders know where to look under pressure.

2
00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:19.304
Consistency is not bureaucracy. It is a way to make different incident scenarios easier to compare, test, and maintain.

3
00:00:19.424 --> 00:00:27.587
The first section is purpose and scope. It explains what scenario the playbook covers and what it does not cover.

4
00:00:27.707 --> 00:00:39.136
For example, a phishing playbook might cover reported suspicious email, confirmed credential harvesting attempts, and malicious attachments, but it may hand off endpoint infection to a malware playbook.

5
00:00:39.256 --> 00:00:44.153
Clear scope prevents teams from forcing every event into the wrong workflow.

6
00:00:44.273 --> 00:00:59.784
Triggering conditions explain when to start. Triggers can include an alert, a user report, an external notification, a suspicious login pattern, a malware detection, a data loss prevention event, an exposed cloud key, or a business owner escalation.

7
00:00:59.904 --> 00:01:03.985
A trigger does not prove an incident. It starts triage.

8
00:01:04.105 --> 00:01:14.718
Severity guidance then helps responders classify impact and urgency. Severity should consider affected users, systems, data, business services, regulatory concerns, active attacker behavior, and containment urgency.

9
00:01:14.838 --> 00:01:28.715
Roles and responsibilities are the next anchor. The playbook should identify who triages, who approves containment, who performs technical actions, who handles legal or privacy review, who updates stakeholders, and who owns recovery decisions.

10
00:01:28.835 --> 00:01:42.713
Required tools and data sources should be listed nearby. These may include endpoint telemetry, authentication logs, cloud audit logs, email security data, network logs, ticketing systems, asset inventory, backup systems, or case management records.

11
00:01:42.833 --> 00:01:55.078
The response actions are the body of the playbook. They should be written as steps that a trained responder can follow, but they should not pretend every branch is automatic.

12
00:01:55.198 --> 00:02:02.544
Some steps are required, such as opening a case, preserving key evidence, recording timestamps, and identifying affected assets.

13
00:02:02.664 --> 00:02:12.460
Some steps are optional branches, such as collecting a memory image only when the host is high value and the organization has that capability.

14
00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:21.152
Some steps are judgment-based decisions, such as whether to disable an account immediately or preserve access briefly to understand scope.

15
00:02:21.272 --> 00:02:37.190
Decision points should be visible. A decision point might ask whether the incident is active, whether sensitive data is involved, whether containment will disrupt a critical service, whether legal review is required, or whether escalation to executives is needed.

16
00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:47.922
Evidence handling notes should tell responders what to collect, where to store it, and how to document chain of custody when formal evidence handling is required.

17
00:02:48.042 --> 00:02:54.573
Finally, the playbook needs communication paths, containment and recovery options, closure criteria, and post-incident tasks.

18
00:02:54.693 --> 00:03:07.346
Closure should require more than "the alert stopped. " It should confirm that the root cause or enabling condition was addressed, affected systems recovered, stakeholders updated, documentation completed, and improvement actions assigned.

19
00:03:07.466 --> 00:03:12.364
A strong playbook guides the incident from first signal to lessons learned.
